    "Stem": "Verde Supply Co. sources cocoa beans from 4,200 smallholder farms across Ghana and Cote d'Ivoire for its chocolate manufacturing operations. The company's sustainability program requires tracing each cocoa shipment from farm to factory to verify fair-trade pricing, organic certification, and deforestation-free sourcing commitments. The current traceability system uses paper-based records at the farm level (recorded by cooperative purchasing agents), entered into a centralized SQL database at regional collection centers, with data uploaded to Verde's SAP system during monthly consolidation. The CFO, Maria Santos, commissioned a blockchain feasibility assessment that produced the following findings: (Finding 1) Throughput Limitation — the Hyperledger Fabric permissioned blockchain proposed for the project achieved 850 transactions per second in testing; Verde's traceability system requires approximately 9,200 transactions per day (farm-level purchases, quality inspections, shipment consolidations), well within capacity; (Finding 2) Energy Cost — the permissioned blockchain's consensus mechanism (Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance) does not use proof-of-work mining; estimated annual energy cost for the 37 validator nodes (one per cooperative + Verde headquarters) is $24,000, representing 0.03% of the program's $78M annual budget; (Finding 3) Node Governance — the proposal requires cooperatives to operate validator nodes; however, 14 of the 37 cooperatives lack reliable internet connectivity (≥95% uptime requirement), and 6 cooperatives have no IT staff capable of maintaining a blockchain node; (Finding 4) Immutability vs. GDPR — blockchain's immutability means that once a transaction is recorded, it cannot be altered; the EU's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) provides data subjects with a 'right to erasure' (Article 17); cocoa farmers whose personal data (name, farm location, production volume) is recorded on the blockchain would have a legal right to request deletion that the blockchain's architecture cannot accommodate. The CFO must identify which findings represent genuine adoption blockers (must be resolved before proceeding) vs. addressable concerns (can be mitigated with planning). Which findings are genuine adoption blockers?",
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Findings 3 and 4 are genuine adoption blockers. Finding 3 (Node Governance): a permissioned blockchain depends on validator nodes operated by consortium members. If 20 of 37 cooperatives cannot reliably operate validator nodes (due to connectivity or IT capability), the consortium either becomes centralized (Verde and the 17 capable cooperatives control consensus, defeating the purpose of distributed trust) or unreliable (nodes frequently disconnecting during consensus rounds cause transaction delays). This is a structural blocker: the consortium members cannot fulfill their node-operator role. Finding 4 (GDPR Immutability Conflict): while the off-chain-data/on-chain-hash workaround is commonly proposed, it has not been tested in court and the European Data Protection Board has expressed skepticism about whether hashing personal data provides true erasure. For a sustainability program that markets itself on ethical practices, a legally untested workaround for farmers' privacy rights is a reputation risk. This is a legal-regulatory blocker. Findings 1 and 2 are NOT blockers: 850 TPS far exceeds the 9,200 daily transaction requirement, and $24,000 in energy costs at 0.03% of the program budget is commercially immaterial.",
